  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Snell's bolete (Leccinum snellii) is a medium-sized bolete with a reddish-brown cap and pale yellow pores that bruise blue-green when handled. It belongs to the Leccinum genus, commonly known as scaber stalks due to their distinctive scaly stems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Blue-Green Bruising",
      "description": "Pores and flesh turn distinctly blue-green when cut or pressed, a key identification feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Scabby Stem",
      "description": "Whitish to pale stem covered with small dark reddish-brown scales or granules",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Reddish-brown to brick-red cap that may fade with age, typically 2-4 inches in diameter",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Snell's bolete fruits in mixed woodlands and forests of New England, often found near birch, aspen, and coniferous trees during summer and fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other scaber stalks, Snell's bolete has a strong ecological relationship with birch trees, forming mycorrhizal associations that help both the fungus and tree thrive.",
